== THE FIRST STAGE

By this time Christiana had got on her way, and Mercy went along with her: so as they went, her children being there also, Christiana began to discourse. And, Mercy, said Christiana, I take this as an unexpected favor, that you should set out of doors with me to accompany me a little in the way.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Then said young Mercy, (for she was but young,) If I thought it would be to any purpose to go with you, I would never go near the town any more.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Well, Mercy, said Christiana, cast in your lot with me: I well know what will be the end of our pilgrimage: my husband is in a place he would not leave for all the gold in the Spanish mines. Nor shall you be rejected, though you go only upon my invitation. The King, who has sent for me and my children, is one that delights in mercy. Besides, if you will, I will hire you, and you shall go along with me as my servant. Yet we will have all things in common between you and me: only go along with me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: But how shall I be assured that I also should be received? If I had this hope but from one that can tell, I would not hesitate at all, but would go, being helped by Him that can help, though the way were ever so tedious.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Well, loving Mercy, I will tell you what you shall do: go with me to the Wicket-gate, and there I will further inquire for you; and if you do not meet with encouragement there, I will be content that you return to your place: I will also pay you for your kindness which you show to me and my children, in accompanying us in the way as you do.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Then I will go there, and will take what shall follow; and the Lord grant that my lot may fall there, even as the King of heaven shall have his heart upon me.]

Christiana then was glad at heart, not only that she had a companion, but also because she had prevailed with this poor maid to fall in love with her own salvation. So they went on together, and Mercy began to weep. Then said Christiana, Why does my sister weep so?

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Alas! said she, who can help but lament, if he will but rightly consider what a state and condition my poor relations are in, who still remain in our sinful town? And what makes my grief the more heavy is that they have no instructor, nor any to tell them what is to come.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Pity becomes pilgrims; and you weep for your friends, as my good Christian did for me when he left me: he mourned because I would not heed nor regard him; but his Lord and ours gathered up his tears, and put them into his bottle; and now both I and you, and these my sweet babes, are reaping the fruit and benefit of them. I hope, Mercy, that these tears of yours will not be lost; for the truth has said that “those who sow in tears will reap with shouts of joy.” And “he who goes out weeping, bearing a trail of seed, will surely return with shouts of joy, carrying sheaves of grain.” Ps 126:5, 6.]

Then said Mercy,

#quote[“Let the Most Blessed be my guide, \
If it be his blessed will, \
Unto his gate, into his fold, \
Up to his holy hill.]

#quote[And let him never suffer me \
To swerve, or turn aside \
From his free-grace and holy ways, \
Whate’er shall me betide.]

#quote[And let him gather them of mine \
That I have left behind; \
Lord, make them pray they may be thine, \
With all their heart and mind.”]

Now my old friend proceeded, and said, But when Christiana came to the Slough of Despond, she began to hold back; for, said she, this is the place in which my dear husband was nearly smothered with mud. She perceived, also, that notwithstanding the command of the King to make this place good for pilgrims, it was rather worse than before. So I asked if that was true. Yes, said the old gentleman, too true; for there are many who pretend to be the King’s laborers, and who say they are for mending the King’s highways, who bring dirt and dung instead of stones, and so mar instead of mending. Here Christiana therefore, with her boys, stood still. But said Mercy, Come, let us venture; only let us be wary. Then they looked well to their steps, and managed to get staggering over.

Yet Christiana was nearly in, and that not once or twice. Now they had no sooner got over than they thought they heard words that said to them, “Blessed is she who has believed that the Lord’s word to her will be fulfilled.” Luke 1:45.

Then they went on again; and Mercy said to Christiana, If I had as good ground to hope for a loving reception at the Wicket-gate as you, I think no Slough of Despond would discourage me.

Well, said the other, you know your sore, and I know mine; and, good friend, we shall all have enough evil before we come to our journey’s end. For can it be imagined that the people who aim to attain such excellent glories as we do, and who are as envied for that happiness as we are, will not meet with whatever fears and snares, whatever troubles and afflictions, those who hate us can possibly assault us with?

And now Mr. Sagacity left me to dream out my dream by myself. So I thought I saw Christiana, and Mercy, and the boys, all go up to the gate; and when they had come to it, they had a short debate about how they should manage their calling at the gate, and what should be said to him who opened to them. So it was decided, since Christiana was the eldest, that she should knock for entrance, and that she should speak for the rest to him who opened. So Christiana began to knock, and as her poor husband had done, she knocked and knocked again. But instead of anyone answering, they all thought they heard a dog come barking at them; a dog, and a great one too; and this made the women and children afraid. Nor did they dare for a while to knock any more, for fear the mastiff would fly at them. Now, therefore, they were greatly tumbled up and down in their minds, and did not know what to do: knock they dared not, for fear of the dog; go back they dared not, for fear the keeper of that gate would see them as they went, and be offended with them. At last they thought of knocking again, and knocked more vehemently than they had at first. Then the keeper of the gate said, Who is there? So the dog stopped barking, and he opened to them.

Then Christiana bowed low, and said, Let not our Lord be offended with his handmaidens for having knocked at his princely gate. Then the keeper said, Where do you#super[+] come from? And what is it that you would have?

Christiana answered, We have come from where Christian came from, and on the same errand as he; that is, to be, if it shall please you, graciously admitted by this gate into the way that leads to the Celestial City. And I answer, my Lord, in the next place, that I am Christiana, once the wife of Christian, who has now gone above.

With that the keeper of the gate marveled, saying, What, has she now become a pilgrim, who only a little while ago abhorred that life? Then she bowed her head, and said, Yes; and so are these my sweet babes also.

Then he took her by the hand and led her in, and also said, Let the little children come to me; and with that he shut the gate. This done, he called to a trumpeter who was above, over the gate, to welcome Christiana with shouting, and with the sound of the trumpet for joy. So he obeyed, and sounded, and filled the air with his melodious notes.

Now all this while poor Mercy stood outside, trembling and crying, for fear that she was rejected. But when Christiana had gained admittance for herself and her boys, she began to intercede for Mercy.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: And she said, My Lord, I have a companion who is still standing outside, who has come here on the same account as myself: one who is much dejected in her mind, because she comes, as she thinks, without being sent for; whereas I was sent for by my husband’s King to come.]

Now Mercy began to be very impatient, and each minute was as long to her as an hour; so she got in before Christiana could intercede for her more fully, by knocking at the gate herself. And she knocked so loud then that she made Christiana start. Then the keeper of the gate said, Who is there? And Christiana said, It is my friend.

So he opened the gate, and looked out, but Mercy had fallen down outside in a swoon, for she fainted, and was afraid that no gate would be opened to her.

Then he took her by the hand, and said, Damsel, I bid you arise.

Oh, sir, she said, I am faint; there is scarcely any life left in me. But he answered that one once said, “When my soul fainted within me, I remembered the #smallcaps[Lord]; my prayer went up to You, to Your holy temple.” Jonah 2:7. Do not fear, but stand on your feet, and tell me why you have come.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: I have come for that to which I was never invited, as my friend Christiana was. Hers was from the King, and mine was only from her. So I fear I presume.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Keeper]: Did she ask you to come with her to this place?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Yes; and, as my Lord sees, I have come. And if there is any grace and forgiveness of sins to spare, I beg that your poor handmaid may be a partaker of it.]

Then he took her by the hand again, and led her gently in, and said, I pray for all those who believe in me, by whatever means they come to me. Then he said to those who stood by, Fetch something and give it to Mercy to smell, to stop her fainting; so they fetched her a bundle of myrrh, and a while after she revived.

And now Christiana and her boys, and Mercy, were received by the Lord at the head of the way, and spoken to kindly by him. Then they said further to him, We are sorry for our sins, and beg our Lord’s pardon, and further instruction in what we must do.

I grant pardon, he said, by word and deed; by word in the promise of forgiveness, by deed in the way I obtained it. Take the first from my lips with a kiss, and the other as it shall be revealed. Song 1:2; John 20:20.

Now I saw in my dream that he spoke many good words to them, by which they were greatly gladdened. He also took them up to the top of the gate, and showed them by what deed they were saved; and told them also that they would have that sight again as they went along in the way, to their comfort.

So he left them a while in a summer parlor below, where they began to talk among themselves; and Christiana began like this: O how glad I am that we have got in here.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: So you well may be; but I, of all of us, have cause to leap for joy.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: I thought at one time, as I stood at the gate, because I had knocked and no one answered, that all our labor had been lost, especially when that ugly cur made such a heavy barking against us.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: But my worst fear was after I saw that you were taken into his favor, and that I was left behind. Now, I thought, that which is written is fulfilled: “Two women will be grinding at the mill: one will be taken and the other left.” Matt 24:41. I could hardly keep from crying out, Undone! And I was afraid to knock any more; but when I looked up at what was written over the gate, I took courage. I also thought that I must either knock again, or die; so I knocked, but I cannot tell how, for my spirit now struggled between life and death.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Can you not tell how you knocked? I am sure your knocks were so earnest that the very sound of them made me start; I thought I had never heard such knocking in all my life; I thought you would come in by a violent hand, or take the kingdom by storm. Matt 11:12.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Alas! Who, being in my case, could have done anything else? You saw that the door was shut on me, and there was a most cruel dog nearby. Who, I say, that was as faint-hearted as I, would not have knocked with all their might? But please, what did my Lord say to my rudeness? Was he not angry with me?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: When he heard your lumbering noise, he gave a wonderfully innocent smile; I believe what you did pleased him well, for he showed no sign to the contrary. But I marvel in my heart why he keeps such a dog: had I known that before, I would not have had heart enough to venture myself in this manner. But now we are in, we are in, and I am glad with all my heart.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: I will ask, if you please, next time he comes down, why he keeps such a filthy cur in his yard; I hope he will not take it amiss.]

Do so, said the children, and persuade him to hang him; for we are afraid he will bite us when we go from here.

So at last he came down to them again, and Mercy fell to the ground on her face before him, and worshiped, and said, “Let my Lord accept the sacrifice of praise which I now offer to him with the calves of my lips.”

So he said to her, Peace be to you; stand up. But she stayed on her face, and said, “Righteous are You, O #smallcaps[Lord], when I plead with You; yet let me talk with You about Your judgments.” Jer 12:1. Why do you keep so cruel a dog in your yard, at the sight of which women and children such as we are ready to flee from your gate for fear?

He answered and said, That dog has another owner; he is also kept shut up in another man’s ground, only my pilgrims hear his barking; he belongs to the castle which you see there at a distance, but he can come up to the walls of this place. He has frightened many an honest pilgrim from worse to better by the great voice of his roaring. Indeed, he who owns him does not keep him out of any good will to me or mine, but with the intent of keeping the pilgrims from coming to me, and so that they may be afraid to come and knock at this gate for entrance. Sometimes also he has broken out, and has mauled some that I loved; but I bear it all patiently for the present. I also give my pilgrims timely help, so that they are not delivered to his power, to do with them what his doggish nature would prompt him to. But what, my purchased one! I believe that if you had known ever so much beforehand, you would not have been afraid of a dog. The beggars that go from door to door will, rather than lose an expected alms, run the risk of the bawling, barking, and biting too of a dog; and shall a dog, a dog in another man’s yard, a dog whose barking I turn to the profit of pilgrims, keep anyone from coming to me? I deliver them from the lions, and my darling from the power of the dog. Ps 22:21, 22.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Then Mercy said, I confess my ignorance; I spoke what I did not understand; I acknowledge that you do all things well.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Then Christiana began to talk of their journey, and to inquire about the way. So he fed them and washed their feet, and set them in the way of his steps, just as he had dealt with her husband before.]
